  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Objective**\n\nOkay, I\'m now clear on the core request: a hyper-casual mobile web game, specifically \"Chain Reaction\". The MVP constraint -- a single `index.html` file -- is also very clear, which seems like a fun challenge. This single-file requirement focuses the project.\n\n\n**Adapting the Structure**\n\nOkay, the single-file HTML requirement presents a structural conflict, but I\'ve resolved to build the game as a React application within the existing Vite/Tailwind structure. I\'ll focus on a single full-screen Canvas component. I will ensure functionality is identical to the requested single `index.html` setup, and I will output it according to existing tools within the project. The workspace setup is a constraint I need to work with, rather than fight against.\n\n\n**Outlining Game Mechanics**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the core gameplay loop. I envision a canvas-based 2D system with drifting particles that detonate when triggered. Each tap drops a \"Trigger Spark\" which expands, creating a chain reaction. Exploding particles have a distinctive visual and audio feedback, including neon colors, debris, and Web Audio API-driven synth sounds. The goal is to clear a target percentage within a round.\n\n\n**Implementing Core Components**\n\nMy current focus is on the codebase structure. I\'m building out the key components now: `App.tsx` for state management, and `GameCanvas.tsx` for the canvas loop, feeding into `GameEngine.ts` to manage game logic. I\'m prioritizing a clean separation of concerns and considering either a dedicated `GameEngine` class or, for a more React-friendly solution, a custom hook, to handle the `requestAnimationFrame` loop. I\'ve also begun to sketch out audio requirements, and a basic oscillator will be implemented in the `GameEngine`.\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the audio, and the particle logic via a defined `GameEngine` class.\n\n\n**Building the Framework**\n\nOkay, I\'m now structuring the main React application. I am integrating Tailwind UI for the start and game-over screens, along with the game canvas. I\'m focusing on wiring up input handling and the rendering of the canvas. This phase includes outlining the game engine components, managing particles, debris, and floating text. I will be working on collision math, and the game level state tracking. I\'m also planning the audio implementation, specifically the detonation sounds and combo effects.\n\n\n**Refining Audio Implementation**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the audio implementation, specifically the detonation sounds. I\'ve focused on using the Web Audio API to create synth sounds. I have crafted a basic oscillator and gain node to create sounds that scale based on combo. I will utilize pentatonic scale or specific multiplier for a more pleasing sound, rather than chromatic steps. The oscillator frequency will change based on the combo count, adding a dynamic and exciting sound for each blast. I\'m also planning the specific actions needed to create the base files and implement the key components.\n\n\n\n**Structuring the Engine**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the game engine\'s architecture. I\'m focusing on consolidating logical components. I\'m prioritizing the structure of the `Particle` states. The `ACTIVE`, `EXPANDING`, `FROZEN`, `SHRINKING`, and `DEAD` states seem to be logical and will be implemented, and the color palette is in place. When a player taps, a special particle will trigger and act similar to any expanding particle. I\'m considering using a `useRef` directly within the `ChainReaction` component to manage the core game engine.\n\n\n**Refining UI Structure**\n\nOkay, after refining the structure, I\'ve resolved to consolidate the logic. I am going to utilize a single component file, `src/components/Game.tsx`, to encapsulate the core game logic. I have realized that `App.tsx` can house the UI wrapper, and `GameCanvas.tsx` can be the container for the canvas and physics loop. I\'m prioritizing the React screen management, using states for the UI lifecycle. I\'m focusing on a mobile-first UI with a responsive design, and I\'ve started the layout using Tailwind. I will prioritize the `Particle` interface implementation.\n\n\n**Defining Particle States**\n\nI\'m now focusing on particle state transitions and game mechanics. I\'ve defined states like `frozen`, `shrinking`, and `dead`, with corresponding timers and radius checks. I\'m prioritizing collision logic, and considering detonation mechanics with combo tracking, debris spawning, and floating text. I\'m also outlining game-over conditions and win/lose scenarios based on particle states and cleared counts. I\'ve also clarified the file generation needs.\n\n\n**Building the Engine**\n\nI\'m now drafting the core `GameEngine` class in `src/engine.ts`, which handles the level, tap events, updates, and drawing. I\'m focusing on integrating audio functions, specifically the `playDetonateSound`, and defining particle states and their corresponding updates within the engine. I\'ll need to pass in canvas dimensions and callbacks for game events to manage overall game state, but this seems to be coming together.\n\n\n**Refining Mechanics & Checks**\n\nI\'m now implementing the core particle states and transitions, considering animations and ease-out curves for visual polish. I\'m finalizing freeze-time duration calculations for better feedback. The win condition is being refactored based on the logic described. I\'ve also outlined and confirmed the execution plan. The project is almost at an MVP.\n\n\n**Structuring the UI**\n\nI\'m now focusing on UI layers: Start, Game, and Game Over screens. I\'m leaning toward drawing the HUD (levels, score, combo) directly on the canvas for performance, while using React for the overall UI structure, especially for Tailwind styling. The *Share Ghost Link* button is included. I need a solution for the HUD update callback to keep React state in sync for the HUD.\n\n\n**Prioritizing UI Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the HUD drawing directly on the canvas, leveraging React for the start and game-over screens. I will manage the level and score HUD through `fillText`, and I\'m avoiding potential React syncing issues with the core gameplay data. I am using a hybrid approach of canvas and React. I am planning to encapsulate the core audio calls into a module (`src/audio.ts`), and I\'ve started the implementation.\n\n\n**Implementing Audio Calls**\n\nI\'m now integrating the audio calls into the game engine, specifically the detonation sounds. I\'ve encapsulated the audio context management and oscillator logic into a dedicated `audio.ts` module. This module exposes a `playDetonate` function, which now receives the combo value. The function creates and configures the oscillator, applying a gain envelope for the explosion effect. The `scaleNode` calculation, using a pentatonic approximation, is working quite well. The audio integration is now complete.\n\n\n**Finalizing Gameplay Checks**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the core game loop. I\'ve completely integrated the game engine, and I am focused on refining the gameplay. I have implemented a system for particle freezing and shrinking, alongside combo tracking and visual feedback, with a win/lose condition check. I\'m focusing on balancing these elements, and the game is ready for a full QA cycle. The code is ready for the final build.\n\n\n**Refining Core Logic**\n\nI\'m now integrating the `GameEngine` with the React components and finalizing the game flow. I am focusing on input handling. I am also planning the `onEndRound` method and integrating it with the game over logic. I need to handle canvas dimensions and ensure game event callbacks are properly managed to integrate the UI. The core game logic seems to be coming together now.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Components**\n\nI\'m now integrating particle initialization and input handling within the main game loop, ensuring the `handleInput` method correctly spawns expanding particles at the tap location. The loop updates and draws particles, while also checking for the win condition, although its inclusion remains under evaluation. The focus is now on overall code quality.\n\n\n\n**Refining Win Conditions**\n\nI\'m now refining the win condition logic, specifically the percentage target calculation. I\'ve realized the win condition needs to be based on the initial number of drifting particles, which is tracked separately from the total active particles. I\'ve clarified and refined the state names for the particles. The update loop implementation has also been refined. I think I\'m very close to MVP.\n\n\n**Optimizing the Rendering**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the canvas drawing methods. I\'ve integrated a dark background with subtle motion trails, and neon particle rendering with shadows. Debris will be drawn as lines or small circles, and the floating text is integrated. I\'m focusing on the overall polish to enhance the user experience.\n\n\n**Connecting React to Engine**\n\nI\'m now integrating the React components, specifically `src/App.tsx`, with the `GameEngine`. I\'ll use `useEffect` to initialize the engine on game start, managing the game state and UI transitions (`START`, `GAME`, `GAMEOVER`). I\'m focusing on passing state data and callbacks from React to the engine, and ensuring responsiveness across different device sizes. The core logic is ready for integration, and the focus is now on the app.\n\n\n**Connecting the Input**\n\nI\'m now integrating the tap input with the game engine. I\'ve updated the `useEffect` hook to set up the canvas dimensions correctly, using `devicePixelRatio` to handle high-DPI displays. I\'m also ensuring the input handler correctly maps the tap coordinates to the canvas, taking into account the pixel ratio.\n\n\n**Refining HUD Implementation**\n\nI\'m now implementing the HUD directly onto the canvas, including the target progress, and current combo state. I am avoiding React for these frequent updates, due to the need for performance and responsiveness. The current logic will use `fillText` and other canvas drawing functions. I\'ve also integrated the debris particle logic for a more visually engaging experience. This direct rendering approach is the correct way, and now I\'m making progress in making this implementation seamless.\n\n\n**Implementing Visual Polish**\n\nI\'m now implementing debris particle drawing with lines and small circles, and integrating floating text. The visual polish involves adding trails, glows, and shadow effects for enhanced visual appeal. The canvas rendering methods for particles, debris, and floating text are all coming together. I\'m focusing on responsiveness across all devices.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Effects**\n\nI\'m now implementing shadow effects for a neon particle glow and refining debris particle rendering. A \"Tap to Play\" button will initialize audio according to browser policy. The game logic difficulty is set, with increasing particle counts and targets across three levels, so now I am focusing on the user experience.\n\n\n\n**Refining Coordinate Handling**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the coordinate system for consistent behavior across devices. I will be operating the game logic in CSS pixels, and only scaling at the `ctx.arc()` draw calls. This addresses a critical issue that prevented game behavior from changing based on display density. I\'m also now implementing the file generation list.\n\n\\n\\n\n\n**Prioritizing File Generation**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the file generation and implementing the structure within the project. I have finalized the core file list. I will be defining `initAudio` and `playDetonate` in `src/lib/audio.ts`, and the full engine in `src/lib/engine.ts`. The React view will be constructed in `src/App.tsx`. I am now implementing this file structure.\n\n\n**Defining Game Over Logic**\n\nI\'m now implementing the core game rules, including the one-tap-per-round mechanic. The single tap drops a trigger, and the round ends when all explosions are complete. I\'m focusing on the win/lose condition checks based on the target percentage. A visual combo counter will also be implemented.\n\nI\'ve also defined the game-over screen requirements. The final score, peak combo count, and share ghost link functionality is also being implemented.\n\n\n**Mapping the Project**\n\nI am now structuring the core files and establishing the initial engine components, and audio routines, with clear separations. The `src/lib` directory is now created, and I will define `initAudio` and `playDetonate` in `audio.ts` with basic functionality. I\'m also preparing to create the engine interface within `engine.ts` including the state properties of the core Particle class. I will ensure a React-friendly type system.\n\n\n**Refining Physics & States**\n\nOkay, I\'m now refining particle interactions and state transitions. I\'m focusing on collision detection. I\'ll flag explosions, and apply the expanding states at the end of each game frame to prevent order-dependent bugs. I have finalized the core `EXPAND_FRAMES`, `FREEZE_FRAMES`, and `SHRINK_FRAMES` constants to streamline calculations. I\'m focusing on the overall game physics and checks.\n\n\n"
