    thinking: "**Defining Store Items**\n\nI\'ve started sketching out the design for the store and upgrades system. My focus right now is on structuring the values and defining buyable items. For example, I\'m thinking of an \"Extra Sparks Battery\" item, with a starting capacity of 1 spark, and a maximum of 3. Level 1 could be free with 1 spark.\n\n\n**Expanding Item Definitions**\n\nI\'ve been fleshing out more items for the store. I\'ve defined the Quantum Fuel Tank, Catalyst Micro-Spark Radius, Magnet Tractor Beam Strength, and Volatility Injector, along with their respective levels, starting values, and shard costs. I am carefully considering the balance of these upgrades to ensure they provide meaningful progression. The Volatility Injector has a nice flow now.\n\n\n**Defining Cosmetic Themes**\n\nI\'ve added cosmetic particle themes, which seem like a perfect fit for user customization. I have defined a few themes, each with a different shard cost. I have also decided on starting the user with 200 shards to allow them to immediately test purchases and get an idea of the system. I also need to save the current upgrades, currency, and active theme in local storage!\n\n\n**Designing Shop Layout**\n\nI\'ve been thinking about the user interface for the shop. A tab, sliding drawer, or overlay is a strong contender, or maybe a dedicated button right on the hand-held chassis. I think I\'ve landed on a \"QUANTUM CORE SHOP\" button on either the \"START\" or \"ROUND_OVER\" screens, changing the state to a `SHOP` screen. It keeps the design tidy and elegant. Now I have a new `Screen` type: `\'START\' | \'GAME\' | \'ROUND_OVER\' | \'SHOP\'`.\n\n\n**Integrating Upgrades**\n\nI\'m now integrating the upgrades and themes into the game engine at `/src/lib/engine.ts`. Specifically, I will modify the particle speeds based on the game level, which makes the game progressively harder. I\'ve updated the velocity calculation for the particles to include a speed scale that increases as the level rises, and I\'ll consider also scaling the particle size to increase difficulty.\n\n\n**Adjusting Particle Radii**\n\nI\'ve been refining the particle system to enhance gameplay. To increase the need for player skill, I\'m scaling particle radii based on level, specifically decreasing radii for higher levels. I\'m testing the impact of radius scaling on gameplay: at level 10, particle radii will shrink to 3.2px, and I\'ve modified `/src/lib/engine.ts` to implement the new mechanic.\n\nI am investigating how activeTheme overrides the color pallet. I\'m taking a look at `/src/lib/engine.ts` line 47, where the `COLORS` are defined and starting to think about adding a dynamic theme picker into the engine.\n\n\n**Implementing Theme Picker**\n\nI\'ve defined `ParticleTheme` and `THEME_COLORS` to manage cosmetic particle themes. I\'m focusing on integrating a dynamic theme picker into the engine. I can now change the colors based on the `activeTheme`.\n\n\n**Tracking Multiple Sparks**\n\nI\'m now implementing the multiple spark feature. I\'m replacing the `tapped` boolean with `sparksTotal` and `sparksLeft` in `GameUpdate` or `GameStats` interface for tracking. The `dropTriggerSpark` function at line 208 will be updated to handle multiple drops with the addition of these variables. This approach enables a more intuitive and visually clear representation of the available sparks.\n\n\n**Refining Spark Mechanics**\n\nI\'m now refining the multiple spark dropping mechanic to optimize tactical control. I\'m focusing on the interaction between the magnet and multiple sparks, particularly during the transition between sparks. I\'ve updated the `dropTriggerSpark` function. I am considering disabling the magnet once the last spark is ready to be dropped for greater tactical control.\n\n\n**Calculating Round Termination**\n\nI\'ve been examining the game loop termination condition. It\'s determined on line 701 of `src/lib/engine.ts`. The round ends when no \"explosives\" are active and no splitter sparks are present. The `started` flag then transitions to false, and the win/loss status is determined. I also reviewed how the radii are calculated, now that upgrades have a more direct impact.\n\n\n**Refining Round Termination Logic**\n\nI\'ve been improving round termination criteria and conditions. I\'ve considered that the level can end if the clear target is met, AND all particles are clear (Instant Perfect Clear). Or, when there are no explosives and splitter sparks, along with no sparks remaining. I am updating line 701 of `src/lib/engine.ts` to reflect the improvements. Now the `isFinished` boolean accounts for this new logic.\n\n\n**Integrating Upgrade Configurations**\n\nI\'m now integrating the store upgrades into the `GameEngine`. Passing the `StoreUpgrades` config to `startLevel` seems the most logical, as the player might purchase upgrades mid-game. I\'m focusing on lines 69 of the `GameEngine` class to add an `upgrades` and `activeTheme` property, initiated in the constructor or `startLevel`. This is a vital step in passing the upgrades into the gameplay loop.\n\n\n**Defining Upgrade Values**\n\nI\'m now defining the structure for the upgrade configurations, which will be integrated in `src/lib/engine.ts`. Specifically, I\'m working on how upgrades impact the game physics. This includes max magnet fuel, magnet pull strength, spark radius boost, and special spawn rates. For instance, the magnet power will scale pull at line 300, and max magnet fuel will be defined in the `GameEngine` class. I will define `StoreUpgrades` interface to contain the levels.\n\n\n\n"
