  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Difficulty Strategies**\n\nI\'m currently focused on increasing the difficulty of the 30-particle base. The existing version is too easy, guaranteeing perfect clears with the power-ups. I\'m exploring different strategies to add complexity and challenge.\n\n\n**Exploring Difficulty Scaling**\n\nI\'m now investigating dynamic difficulty scaling, and considering multiple avenues. I\'m thinking of increasing particle count and velocity, reducing hitboxes, and shortening shrink times. I am also exploring introducing negative particles, like anti-matter cores, as well as obstacles and hazards such as shield cores or volatile anti-energy cores to create more challenging interactions. Nullifier cores are also on the table, to absorb neighboring explosions or trigger other events.\n\n\n**Introducing Anti-Matter Cores**\n\nI\'ve hit on a promising mechanic: Anti-Matter Cores! These dark red particles absorb explosions, disrupting chain reactions. Now, I\'m thinking of incorporating them, maybe as 10-20% of spawned particles at higher levels. They\'ll demand players carefully manage their particle placement, and herd atoms away from them! I can\'t wait to test their impact.\n\n\n**Refining Anti-Matter Properties**\n\nI\'m now refining the behavior of the Anti-Matter Cores. Initially, I thought about absorption, explosion extinction, or inert absorption. I\'m leaning toward them silently \"popping\" to extinguish nearby explosions. The goal is to provide a tense, high-stakes feel. The next step is a deep dive, to explore how Nullifier or Inert Cores or Heat-Sinks would impact user gameplay. I have to select a color, \"\342\234\226\" or \"-\" orbiter and subtle drain effect, to really hone-in on this design. These cores will prevent easy chain reactions.\n\n\n**Revising Difficulty Scaling**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into velocity scaling, and rethinking the \'Reactor Overload\' mode. The existing formula looks like `1.35 + Math.min(2.1, (level - 1) * 0.16)`. Also, radius scaling is: `Math.max(0.70, 1.0 - (level - 1) * 0.03)`. I also see that upgrades like `extraSparks` effectively guarantee clears. I must make it harder, perhaps by increasing base speed or making target harder.\n\n\n**Adjusting Upgrade Balancing**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on balancing. The current maxRadius of the trigger spark at 80px guarantees easy clears. Upgrades like magnetPower and Gravity/Splitter spawn rates are overwhelming the challenge. It\'s time to reduce the efficacy of upgrades. I need to make sure that the player must use actual skill! I\'m thinking about limiting the impact of those special particles.\n\n\n**Implementing Level Challenges**\n\nI\'m now focusing on specific level challenges and potential mechanics. I\'m increasing the target percentage requirement, and also considering increasing particle speed for dynamic herding. I\'m also planning to incorporate a subtle reactor pressure that increases particle speed or introduces higher-speed particles if players purchase upgrades. Finally, I\'m exploring magnet-related challenges like magnet fuel depletion.\n\n\n**Defining New Challenge Elements**\n\nI\'m now implementing Decay Cores, also known as Anti-Matter Atoms or Heat-Sinks. I\'m exploring \"Turbulence Gravity Waves\" for added challenge. These cores should appear at level 2+, and I\'m looking at their states, which are \'DRIFTING\', \'EXPANDING\', \'FROZEN\', \'SHRINKING\', and \'DEAD\', and their types, \'STANDARD\', \'GRAVITY\', \'SPLITTER\'. I\'m also planning to reduce the blast radius if used in quick succession to maintain a difficulty curve.\n\n\n**Integrating Decay Cores**\n\nI\'m now incorporating Decay Cores, also known as Anti-Matter Atoms, that absorb explosions and extinguish reactions. These cores, visually deep crimson, will scale with level, starting at 10% of standard particles at level 2. Their behavior is now defined: when hit, they absorb and shrink other particles, preventing chain reactions. I\'m keen to test how these will require strategic particle management and herding.\n\n\n**Implementing Turbulence Winds**\n\nI\'m now integrating \"Reactor Turbulence\" via periodic magnetic solar waves. These forces, starting at level 3, will disrupt particle clusters, demanding precise timing and reactions from the player. I envision a visual warning banner displaying a red/yellow laser-like wave for the \"drift force\" effect.\n\n\n**Defining Decay Particle Colors**\n\nI\'m now integrating decay particles. After reviewing `/src/lib/engine.ts`, I\'ve adjusted the `ParticleType` to include \'DECAY\'. I\'ve updated the theme colors, adding a `DECAY` key with dark, threatening colors \342\200\223 initially using a deep radioactive red. I\'m focusing on defining the visual representation to match the challenging behavior of these new particles.\n\n\n**Defining Decay Colors**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the engine\'s particle definitions in `/src/lib/engine.ts`. Specifically, I\'ve confirmed `ParticleType` includes \'DECAY\' and I will create the `ParticleTheme` with a dark radioactive color. I am planning on defining the visual representation to represent the challenge.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 21
      nanos: 544422259
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
