  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing `startLevel` Data**\n\nI\'m making good progress analyzing the `startLevel` data within `/src/lib/engine.ts`, focusing on the critical lines 210-251. I\'m noting the difficulty upgrade mechanics, specifically velocity scaling. I\'m focusing on those specific lines to understand the function of the upgrades.\n\n\n**Examining Particle Spawn Logic**\n\nI\'m now diving into the particle spawning logic, specifically how `DECAY` cores are introduced based on level progression. I\'ve noted the scaling of the velocity multiplier and hitbox radius, and I\'m interested in the special particle spawn rate. I am focusing on the probability-based logic and theme colors as well.\n\n\n**Implementing Decay Core Logic**\n\nI\'ve decided to implement `DECAY` core introduction as a percentage of particles, scaling with level. At level 2, it\'s 8%, increasing to 24% at level 10. I am using `decayRatio` for this calculation. The distribution logic will be incorporated into the particle spawning `for` loop, using `Math.random()`. I like that this is elegant, and will require no refactoring. `this.totalDrifting` specifies the number of normal particles to clear.\n\n\n**Refining Target Calculation**\n\nI\'ve realized a critical flaw in my target percentage calculation. The current system doesn\'t account for `DECAY` cores, which are unclearable and thus impede progress toward the target percentage. To rectify this, I propose a few solutions. The cleanest solution appears to be keeping `this.totalDrifting` as a count of only **clearable** particles. This simplifies the percentage calculation, as `DECAY` cores won\'t be factored into the required percentage. The number of `DECAY` cores will then be added separately.\n\n\n**Validating Decay Core Logic**\n\nI\'ve just validated the `DECAY` core logic, specifically the calculation based on `level` and `this.totalDrifting`. The formula elegantly determines the number of decay cores, ensuring levels remain winnable, even with those cores present. The target percentage calculation remains clean, and the `DECAY` core count is handled separately, maintaining the integrity of the clearable particle count. This elegant calculation is perfect, and also supports my theory!\n\nI\'m now investigating \"Reactor Turbulence\" waves, which will be introduced at level 3. I am interested in how the `update()` function will introduce a turbulence wave, tracking its `turbulenceTimer`, and animating its visual effect on the canvas with gorgeous neon styling. The waves can be periodic, appearing every 8-12 seconds.\n\n\n**Designing Turbulence Waves**\n\nI\'m now designing the \"Reactor Turbulence\" waves, focusing on their visual impact and functional effect. I\'ll store `turbulenceX`, `turbulenceActive`, and `turbulenceDirection` to manage the wave\'s position and state. My plan involves a sweeping wave from left to right, affecting particles within a 50px band, applying a directional drift force, and this will be visually stunning! I will need to check how the existing `update()` and `draw()` functions operate within `engine.ts`.\n\n\n**Handling Decay Collisions**\n\nI\'m now focused on the collision detection logic, specifically lines 480-515. I\'m considering the edge case when a `DECAY` core collides with an `EXPANDING` particle. Currently, drifting particles are set to `EXPANDING` on collision, but what is the right interaction for a `DECAY` core? Should it have different behavior, or be ignored? This requires careful design.\n\n\n**Implementing Dampening Logic**\n\nI\'ve decided on the `DECAY` core collision behavior! A collision with an explosive hub will trigger a dampening shockwave! The decay core absorbs the explosive hub\'s energy, immediately shrinking it and halting its expansion. The code will be: `exp.state = \'SHRINKING\'; exp.radius = Math.max(0.5, exp.radius * 0.5);`. This is an incredibly elegant heat-sink mechanic, killing the chain reaction branching from that explosive cell! I am excited by how this impacts gameplay.\n\n\n**Verifying Spark Interactions**\n\nI\'m now verifying the interaction of splitter sparks with `DECAY` cores. My thought is that upon collision, splitter sparks should be silently dissolved, and this is now verified! This happens within the collision check, where `s.life = 0` is set if a `DECAY` core is hit. The sparks are then properly dissipated, and I am satisfied with this aspect of the game design!\n\n\n**Refining Core Interactions**\n\nI\'m now refining the collision check, especially the interaction of `DECAY` cores with explosives. I was amazed at how a simple dampening effect could add a tactical layer to gameplay. I\'m focusing on the `spawnDecayAbsorbJuice` function, and how it should spawn a visually arresting particle cloud with accompanying popup text. My intent is to make the player feel rewarded for this interaction!\n\n\n**Considering Trigger Logic**\n\nI\'m now contemplating how trigger sparks work in relation to `DECAY` cores. I realized players can click on a `DECAY` core to spawn a trigger spark, which is an expanding white circle. This is a game design consideration.\n\n\n**Evaluating Core Magnetism**\n\nI\'m now evaluating the effect of the herding magnet on `DECAY` cores. I noticed they\'re currently pulled, which creates interesting gameplay implications. Should they be herded? This could be a tactical option. This opens strategic possibilities for players, which could be positive for the game.\n\n\n**Adjusting Herding Speed**\n\nI\'m now implementing the herding mechanic for `DECAY` cores. Initially, I was thinking about making them fully repellant. I am now considering a reduced herding force multiplier, and I\'ve decided on a lower herding speed multiplier (e.g., 0.4x). This will be determined by dense \"anti-matter\" mass. I\'ve considered that they are heavier, or drift faster.\n\n\n**Implementing Turbulence Physics**\n\nI\'m now implementing the herding mechanic for `DECAY` cores. I have fully incorporated the gravitational turbulence waves, and I\'m very happy with how it\'s working! I\'ve been refining the logic and visuals, ensuring the waves provide a unique challenge. DECAY particles are heavy, which provides tactical advantages to players. I\'ve designed a great wave with unique animation!\n\n\n\n**Incorporating Turbulence Wave**\n\nI\'m now incorporating a beautiful energy wave, creating a visually stunning effect! I\'m planning a vertical matrix scanning line or wave ripple, maybe glowing neon crimson or warning orange. I\'m investigating how to insert this logic within the `update()` or `draw()` functions to give that rippling wave effect. I want to enhance gameplay and provide players with visual feedback!\n\n\n\n**Refining Decay Core Spawning**\n\nI\'m now refining the spawning of `DECAY` cores. I am now considering the decay core spawning ratio calculation. I\'ve been considering how to adjust the total particle count in relation to the level, considering the introduction of `DECAY` cores. I am using `Math.min()` to clamp the calculation to prevent the level from becoming unwinnable. I want the mechanic to be an interesting challenge.\n\n\n**Optimizing Particle Spawning**\n\nI\'m now optimizing the particle spawning logic, focusing on replacing the existing loop with separate loops for standard and `DECAY` core spawning. I have been re-evaluating lines 220-246, making sure to preserve the special particle spawn logic and introducing a new `DECAY` core loop!\n\n\n**Refining Spawning Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the particle spawning loops. I\'m separating the standard and `DECAY` core spawning loops for clarity and control. The `decayCount` is determined, and now I\'m generating them separately. I\'m focusing on ensuring proper distribution and visual cues. My focus is performance and visual impact!\n\n\n**Implementing Decay Core Movement**\n\nI\'m now implementing `DECAY` core movement, specifically their unique properties within the game\'s physics. I\'ve increased their radius slightly for a visual match. I am setting their `maxRadius` to zero since they don\'t detonate. The current thought is to update turbulence within `update()`, specifically around lines 351-436.\n\n\n**Adjusting Decay Core Behavior**\n\nI\'m now modifying the herding magnet\'s effect on `DECAY` cores. I\'ve realized I need to modify the particle loop to account for the unique physics. I\'ve been adjusting the `update()` function, specifically regarding the herding force application. The logic needs to be distinct from other particles.\n\n\n**Integrating Turbulence Waves**\n\nI\'m now integrating the turbulence wave logic within the `update()` function, specifically lines 376-377. I\'m focusing on the warning phase and activation, using timers for the periodic wave effects. The wave will sweep across the screen, applying a directional force to drifting particles.\n\n\n"
